420 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (Then comes the act itself, and it is usually unremarkable. Rakul took a genuine supplier that had been inactive for months. He changed its registered bank account to one he controlled. Whenever a legitimate invoice arrived, he routed the payment there.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (There were no hacked systems and no forged signatures at the moment of payment. It looked like routine vendor maintenance.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (That is what makes many workplace frauds difficult to spot. Investigators may find no dramatic system breach or obviously fabricated invoice. Instead, the warning sign sits in a seemingly ordinary change to supplier information and a transaction that looks reasonable when viewed in isolation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (In this case, the question was whether the bank details were legitimate, who had changed them, and whether that change had been independently verified. Following the money matters, but so does tracing the decisions and changes that allowed it to move.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
