502 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 235 0 R
/T (3. Many processes rely on employees following procedures correctly. How can organisations reduce their dependence on individual judgement without unnecessarily complicating processes?)
/E (3. Many processes rely on employees following procedures correctly. How can organisations reduce their dependence on individual judgement without unnecessarily complicating processes?)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
503 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (The goal is not to eliminate human judgement, but to reduce redundant discretion and make the right action the easiest action.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Organisations can achieve this through clear accountability, role-based access, segregation of duties and risk-based approvals. Technology can strengthen these controls by automatically flagging unusual transactions, supplier changes and recurring exceptions.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (The principle is simple: simplify routine decisions, automate predictable controls and reserve human judgement for higher-risk exceptions. Good controls should make processes safer, not slower.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Similarly, employees may be following the existing policies correctly, but that does not necessarily mean those policies are addressing every relevant risk. This is why professional scepticism is so important.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Organisations should look at what was tested, whether earlier issues were genuinely resolved and whether new risks have emerged since the last review. It is critical to constantly review the organisation risk management process to ensure new and emerging risk are assessed and impacted in to policies and procedures.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (I endorse leaders regularly questioning whether existing controls remain fit for purpose and identify any unusual activity that may require closer scrutiny.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 235 0 R
/T (4. Can high policy compliance or a clean audit report sometimes create misplaced confidence? How should organisations interpret these signals?)
/E (4. Can high policy compliance or a clean audit report sometimes create misplaced confidence? How should organisations interpret these signals?)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Yes, absolutely. A clean audit report is encouraging, but it should not be treated as a guarantee that there is no fraud. Every audit has a defined scope, and there may be areas or transactions that were not examined in detail.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 235 0 R
/E (Sometimes, the problem is not that controls are missing, but that people have become too comfortable with the way things are done. Organisations need to keep asking questions and address weaknesses instead of assuming that existing processes are working perfectly. Finally, when the underlying governance is weak , reporting can show all green and the control incidents may never get the attention its needed, therefore awareness on what are the key metrics and transparency on how is it reported is crucial to avoid false sense of security.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 235 0 R
/T (5. When an investigation uncovers fraud, how can organisations distinguish between an isolated act of misconduct and a deeper weakness in the process or control environment?)
/E (5. When an investigation uncovers fraud, how can organisations distinguish between an isolated act of misconduct and a deeper weakness in the process or control environment?)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
