>>
endobj
526 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 236 0 R
/T (How to Break the Illusion)
/E (How to Break the Illusion)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
527 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 236 0 R
/T (Why Controls Fail)
/E (Why Controls Fail)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
528 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (The illusion often develops through everyday practices. A signature or system log may prove that an action occurred, but not that the person involved identified a problem. Similarly, a well-written procedure means little if employees do not follow it properly. Managers reviewing hundreds of invoices may start approving them without meaningful checks, while shared logins, delegated passwords and excessive access can undermine the separation of duties.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
529 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (Another weakness is relying on limited samples to assess controls. Reviewing a handful of transactions may not reveal repeated irregularities across thousands of records. The common issue is that organisations often check whether controls exist without asking whether they can actually prevent or detect a loss.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
530 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (The answer is not simply to add more controls. It is to make existing controls more effective by testing them against the risks they are meant to address.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
531 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (Start by asking how someone could steal, conceal a transaction or manipulate records. Then assess whether the controls would stop or expose that activity. Do not stop at reviewing policies; re-perform selected checks and see whether a reviewer could identify an error or suspicious transaction.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
532 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (Data analytics can help uncover patterns that manual reviews may miss. Reviewers should also be accountable for their judgment, not just their signatures. Ask them to document what they checked and what concerns they raised.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
533 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 236 0 R
/E (Finally, measure whether controls are producing the intended results. Track losses, write-offs, credit notes, unusual voids and complaints. If these increase while controls are reported as effective, investigate whether those controls are working as intended.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
534 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 236 0 R
/T (The Question That Matters:)
/E (The Question That Matters:)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
